WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
A soldier that was in the Crimean War…

00:06.000 --> 00:11.000
he was called John the Soldier. 

00:14.000 --> 00:17.000
I remember about the soldier, he was wounded, 

00:17.000 --> 00:20.000
I think it was the Crimean, that war I don’t remember right. 

00:20.000 --> 00:24.000
He had the bone of the eyebrow cut off, 

00:24.000 --> 00:27.000
that was kept in a silk handkerchief to go into the coffin 

00:27.000 --> 00:30.000
along with him when he would be buried. 

00:31.000 --> 00:33.000
And he had an honour, by saving the [fort/?], 

00:33.000 --> 00:36.000
he could produce his sword at anytime, 

00:36.000 --> 00:39.000
an honour from royalty, the king I suppose. 

00:39.000 --> 00:43.000
At the time of the Clearances, he was going to be evicted along with the rest. 

00:44.000 --> 00:49.000
While he was waiting for the [XX] lawyers coming 

00:49.000 --> 00:54.000
and so on to clear them out, to clear them out of the house, 

00:54.000 --> 00:56.000
himself and his family. 

00:56.000 --> 00:58.000
And he had this overcoat on and he was 

00:58.000 --> 01:01.000
fully-armed with the sword, and he just showed them the sword. 

01:01.000 --> 01:05.000
“Come off that road,” he says, “Come off that track, 

01:05.000 --> 01:09.000
or I will make widows of the wives of every one of you.”

01:09.000 --> 01:13.000
They bypassed him and he was taken to court in Lochmaddy 

01:13.000 --> 01:17.000
because he was, you know, against them. And the sheriff asked him:

01:17.000 --> 01:20.000
“Did you produce a sword to these people 

01:20.000 --> 01:24.000
when they tried to enter your house?” And he says,

01:24.000 --> 01:29.000
“I did, and I’ve got full permission to produce it in any public place, 

01:29.000 --> 01:33.000
and as a matter of fact, it’s here and your head 

01:33.000 --> 01:36.000
will be the first one if I’m cleared out.

01:36.000 --> 01:39.000
He said that to the sheriff. Oh, he could do it 

01:39.000 --> 01:44.000
because he was decorated in the Crimean War. 

01:44.000 --> 01:47.000
So he was never evicted, he was never put out. 

01:48.000 --> 01:51.000
Another version of this story that I heard in Creagorry, 

01:51.000 --> 01:56.000
Benbecula from Mr MacKenzie, a retired minister was the same 

01:56.000 --> 01:59.000
in the essentials but added the fine details that 

01:59.000 --> 02:04.000
the Crimean hero was always called ‘Iain Saighdear’ – John the Soldier. 

02:04.000 --> 02:16.000
‘Iain Saighdear’ had strutted to and fro brandishing his sabre along [XX]

02:16.000 --> 02:18.000
thickness of the walls of his black house, 

02:18.000 --> 02:21.000
a favourite place for householders to sit, 

02:21.000 --> 02:24.000
and take their ease, that he had said:

02:25.000 --> 02:31.000
“I would never put the sword back in it’s scabbard but it was running with blood.”

02:32.000 --> 02:37.000
Considered as a story, it’s a grand legend of triumphant defiance. 

02:37.000 --> 02:40.000
He was always at this legend, this man. 

02:40.000 --> 02:48.000
[...] 

02:49.000 --> 02:55.000
But triumphant defiance, considered as history, it is riddled with total [XX]. 

02:55.000 --> 03:01.000
If the war in which Iain Saighdear had done heroic things was the Crimean War, 

03:01.000 --> 03:06.000
it would have been a queen, not a king, who awarded the honour, 

03:06.000 --> 03:10.000
and it cannot have happened during the Clearances, 

03:10.000 --> 03:14.000
the Crimean War by five years, he’s doubting everything, this man.

03:14.000 --> 03:19.000
Yes he is, oh I don’t want to hear anymore of him. I want to hear about you.

03:20.000 --> 03:24.000
No, he’s not of very … important. 

03:25.000 --> 03:28.000
He was writing a book for money, that was about it.

03:28.000 --> 03:30.000
Aye, I’m not into that.

03:32.000 --> 03:39.000
And, oh there were tales of MacCodrum but there was more of it in poetry. 

03:40.000 --> 03:44.000
I used to have that book but it’s out of print now. 

03:45.000 --> 03:52.000
But this Willie Matheson, he was a… in the School of Scottish Studies, 

03:52.000 --> 03:55.000
he was in fact a minister for a wihle, 

03:55.000 --> 04:03.000
then he entered the School of Scottish Studies and he had, oh he had a lot of talk. 

04:03.000 --> 04:08.000
In fact, there’s a book about Willie Matheson …

04:08.000 --> 04:13.000
but that’s about the clans, you know, and the relations of this and that 

04:13.000 --> 04:16.000
and the MacRuries and the MacDonald and …

04:16.000 --> 04:20.000
but where is it I couldn’t put my finger on it at the moment.

04:20.000 --> 04:25.000
Och that’s alright, that’s alright. I’m not so much interested in the family history.

04:24.000 --> 04:25.000
No, no.

04:25.000 --> 04:30.000
I’m more interested in just the stories that have been passed down…

04:30.000 --> 04:31.000
Passed down?

04:31.000 --> 04:36.000
About so and so, and such and such, and about this happening and this happening.

04:36.000 --> 04:37.000
Oh, happenings like that.

04:37.000 --> 04:47.000
Yes, aye, and the manadh, second sight. Foresight, when there’s a premonition, aye.

04:46.000 --> 04:47.000
Aye, oh well.

04:47.000 --> 04:50.000
I’ve heard some good ones from Mary [XX] down in Stoneybridge.

04:50.000 --> 04:56.000
Oh yes I’m sure, I’m sure. They’re more to that side than we in North Uist are.

04:56.000 --> 04:57.000
Aye, are they, aye?

04:57.000 --> 05:01.000
That’s what they call, I’m sure you’re a Catholic yourself, are you?

05:01.000 --> 05:03.000
No, I’m Protestant.

05:03.000 --> 05:06.000
Protestant? Oh well, they say these Catholics, you know, 

05:06.000 --> 05:11.000
they believe in more than they do down here, I don’t know why. 

05:11.000 --> 05:16.000
Because the Protestant religion, you know, the ministers and the likes 

05:16.000 --> 05:21.000
were so far against this, the second sight and… 

05:21.000 --> 05:26.000
that it was just a fairytale, as you would say. 

05:27.000 --> 05:33.000
And then about the [dwarfs/?] in the hillocks, called sìthichean, 

05:35.000 --> 05:42.000
in my young days they would be telling you about these sìthichean, 

05:42.000 --> 05:45.000
they were in this hillock and that hillock and all that and… 

05:45.000 --> 05:50.000
a woman that, I’m sure you heard that in South Uist too, 

05:50.000 --> 05:55.000
a woman who heard the music when she went for the cattle…

05:54.000 --> 05:56.000
No, I didn’t.

05:57.000 --> 06:01.000
And uh, when she went to the cattle, where the cattle, 

06:01.000 --> 06:04.000
she heard the music in the hill and she went over to the, this, 

06:05.000 --> 06:10.000
she saw a wee hole there and she went in there among the fairies. 

06:11.000 --> 06:16.000
And she was there for 20 years, and she thought, when she came out, 

06:16.000 --> 06:18.000
that she was just for a day in there. 

06:19.000 --> 06:23.000
Because, and they gave her the job of making baking, 

06:24.000 --> 06:32.000
and they told her to… every time what she left over 

06:32.000 --> 06:36.000
had to be put back into the meal chest. 

06:37.000 --> 06:42.000
But, oh well she would never get out, because it was returning 

06:42.000 --> 06:45.000
to the meal chest all the time... 

06:46.000 --> 06:50.000
But, oh perhaps I’ve got the story the wrong way. 

06:51.000 --> 06:56.000
She went out to bake and also [XX] scones and the likes, 

06:56.000 --> 07:02.000
but the old man that was in, the others were out:

07:02.000 --> 07:06.000
“You put it back,” he says, “into the meal chest, 

07:06.000 --> 07:10.000
what’s left over, and you’ll soon get out of here.”

07:10.000 --> 07:16.000
And that’s what happened. And when they came home, she was away.

07:16.000 --> 07:22.000
And she thought she was only a day away from home, 

07:22.000 --> 07:27.000
and she was for 20 years in that hill. [...] 

07:27.000 --> 07:30.000
Very difficult to believe these things.

07:31.000 --> 07:33.000
Och aye, aye, but it’s fun, you know.

07:34.000 --> 07:37.000
It’s interesting to see what people come up with and... 

07:38.000 --> 07:42.000
aye, you wonder what the kernel of truth was in that one.

07:42.000 --> 07:43.000
Eh?

07:43.000 --> 07:47.000
You know, what was the kernel of truth? Because every story starts somewhere.

07:47.000 --> 07:50.000
Yes, yes, starts somewhere. 

07:50.000 --> 07:55.000
Oh, perhaps that was a story where somebody that was a baby, 

07:55.000 --> 07:59.000
well not a baby but year old, years, that wouldn’t sleep 

07:59.000 --> 08:05.000
and they would tell the story and the baby would be away, 

08:06.000 --> 08:09.000
as they say, away with the fairies. 

08:11.000 --> 08:16.000
But as for MacCodrum I haven’t that much about him at all,

08:16.000 --> 08:18.000
if I had the book, you know…

08:18.000 --> 08:21.000
Aye, well that’s alright, I can come back sometime too.

08:20.000 --> 08:21.000
Yes.

08:21.000 --> 08:24.000
I’ll be back, I’ll be here until the end of July.

08:24.000 --> 08:28.000
You’ll be here until the end of July?

08:27.000 --> 08:28.000
Mhm.

08:29.000 --> 08:33.000
I don’t know where I could find that book at all. 

08:34.000 --> 08:55.000
[PREVIOUS CLIP REPLAYS] 

08:55.000 --> 09:00.000
That book was uh… it was a dead thing, you know.

09:00.000 --> 09:02.000
Oh aye. Who wrote it?
Pardon?

09:02.000 --> 09:04.000
Who wrote the book?

09:04.000 --> 09:07.000
Well it was this William Matheson I was telling you about from the 

09:07.000 --> 09:11.000
School of Scottish Studies in 1936, he had his... 

09:11.000 --> 09:15.000
he was going to college, and he was seven weeks, 

09:15.000 --> 09:21.000
he had seven weeks to [do up/?] and he was cycling up to South Uist for… 

09:21.000 --> 09:24.000
did you ever hear of that…? 

09:25.000 --> 09:32.000
Oh, that was a great story teller, Duncan MacDonald of Penirine. 

09:29.000 --> 09:32.000
Yes aye.

09:32.000 --> 09:37.000
Yes, and his son was a bard, Donald John, he died recently too.

09:37.000 --> 09:40.000
Yes aye, I saw his house today.

09:39.000 --> 09:40.000
Eh?

09:40.000 --> 09:42.000
I saw his house today.

09:42.000 --> 09:46.000
Yes, it’s a pity he wasn’t, you weren’t here…

09:46.000 --> 09:48.000
I know. Late.

09:48.000 --> 09:50.000
Ohh, you would get a lot from him.

09:49.000 --> 09:50.000
Aye.

09:53.000 --> 09:58.000
There’s another fellow there, MacMillan, he’s got a lot of stories.

09:58.000 --> 09:59.000
Where’s he?

09:59.000 --> 10:00.000
Eh?

10:00.000 --> 10:02.000
Where does he live?

10:02.000 --> 10:04.000
Did you see him?

10:04.000 --> 10:06.000
No, I don’t know, is he South Uist?

10:05.000 --> 10:06.000
Aye.

10:06.000 --> 10:09.000
MacMillan? No, I haven’t seen him.

10:09.000 --> 10:12.000
What’s his name? But MacRury will know if you see him.

10:12.000 --> 10:17.000
Is there a Hugh Matheson? Hugh Matheson?

10:17.000 --> 10:21.000
Oh, in Baleshare? [Ruairidh/?] [XX].

10:21.000 --> 10:24.000
I haven’t seen him yet, but I’ve heard that he might have a few stories.

10:24.000 --> 10:27.000
Oh yes he could have a few stories.

10:28.000 --> 10:34.000
And I went to see, uh, Donald Ailick MacIsaac yesterday, up in Kirkibost.

10:34.000 --> 10:36.000
Oh aye, he has got a lot of stories.

10:36.000 --> 10:40.000
Aye, but he has a hard time remembering. You know, he’s not feeling well, 

10:40.000 --> 10:41.000
he was having a hard time remembering.

10:41.000 --> 10:43.000
Oh no, he’s just a cripple now.

10:43.000 --> 10:45.000
Yeah, he’s having a hard time.

10:47.000 --> 10:53.000
He’s just a cripple. Donald Ailick was in the war and he’s got rheumatics and oh...

10:54.000 --> 10:58.000
Yes, yes.

11:00.000 --> 11:07.000
But about Jessie of Balranald, she had, well I’m sure MacRury has got… 

11:09.000 --> 11:11.000
I’m telling her about Jessie of Balranald.

11:12.000 --> 11:16.000
There was a crowd from Skye, the stalwarts, 

11:16.000 --> 11:22.000
and they took the mast out of the boat and rammed the front door with the mast. 

11:23.000 --> 11:30.000
Up they went and they were tying them up, the servants and everyone that was … 

11:31.000 --> 11:33.000
they tied them up, you know. 

11:33.000 --> 11:38.000
Even the guns were out, but they never killed anybody. 

11:39.000 --> 11:47.000
And he took her out of bed, she was sleeping with her uncle’s wife, 

11:48.000 --> 11:52.000
that was the bed she got. He took her out of there. 

11:52.000 --> 11:57.000
But a maid was, a maid in the house gave them the information, 

11:57.000 --> 12:01.000
I think she was from Skye herself, and … 

12:04.000 --> 12:07.000
she gave them the information about Jessie, 

12:07.000 --> 12:10.000
where she slept and all. 

12:11.000 --> 12:15.000
And there were eight, eight stalwarts from Skye that took her down

12:15.000 --> 12:18.000
to the boat and away they went. 

12:18.000 --> 12:21.000
They got away this time and married, 

12:21.000 --> 12:24.000
I think, in Glasgow, and went to Astràilia. 

12:25.000 --> 12:33.000
They landed there, they landed in Astràilia and one day – 

12:34.000 --> 12:37.000
she must have been a beautiful woman – 

12:38.000 --> 12:43.000
one day one of the Maouris came round with a pony and she was 

12:43.000 --> 12:46.000
in on her own, and he ran away with her. 

12:47.000 --> 12:52.000
But this Donald was, there was a maid in the house and he said:

12:52.000 --> 12:57.000
“Well, which way did he go?” And she told him that he went that way, 

12:57.000 --> 13:01.000
and he went the other way, and she was… 

13:01.000 --> 13:04.000
she was in front of him on the horse, 

13:04.000 --> 13:07.000
and he lifted his gun and killed the Maouri outright, 

13:07.000 --> 13:09.000
and she, in his arms. 

13:11.000 --> 13:13.000
And he got her that time. 

13:14.000 --> 13:17.000
So that was the end of that story. 

13:17.000 --> 13:20.000
Some people were saying that they saw their grandchildren in … 

13:21.000 --> 13:24.000
but I wouldn’t give two hoots for that!

13:24.000 --> 13:26.000
They saw their grandchildren?

13:26.000 --> 13:30.000
That they saw their grandchildren of them in Astràilia.

13:28.000 --> 13:30.000
Oh aye, aye.

13:30.000 --> 13:38.000
I’m under [XX] here... I’m under [XX] here, because...

13:38.000 --> 13:41.000
Is this, is this him? Is that the man?

13:41.000 --> 13:46.000
That’s the man, Craig. David Craig, he was born in Aberdeen, 1932.

13:46.000 --> 13:50.000
Now seems to come with the writer Anne Spillard. 

13:51.000 --> 13:54.000
He teaches Creative Writing at [Lancaster/?] University 

13:54.000 --> 13:57.000
and has published books of poetry and fiction.

13:58.000 --> 14:02.000
[...]

14:02.000 --> 14:05.000
Oh, that’s a lot Maggie.

14:05.000 --> 14:25.000
[Discussion about tea break]

14:25.000 --> 14:26.000
Have you ever heard of the … 

14:26.000 --> 14:28.000
St Bernard in Nova Scotia

14:28.000 --> 14:30.000
Oh, it’s beautiful.

14:31.000 --> 14:36.000
1920, the last inhabitant of Pabbay in Harris.

14:37.000 --> 14:40.000
He must have been an Englishman. 

14:41.000 --> 14:46.000
That was the last resident in Pabbay, that’s an island just of the Isle…

14:46.000 --> 14:53.000
Aye, I know it, I know Pabbay. Haven’t been though. Going to go to Berneray next week.

14:53.000 --> 15:01.000
Oh yes, it’s very easy, very easy to go to Berneray once you get to the jetty. 

15:03.000 --> 15:10.000
That’s Betty Fraser, Gartymore, about 1910, evicted from Costly, Kildonan in [1819/?]. 

15:11.000 --> 15:18.000
Minnie Campbell, Gills, evicted from Grummore, Strath Naver, 1819. 

15:21.000 --> 15:29.000
Tarvaskaig- Tarskavaig, in Skye, in 1931. That’s the crofts of Skye.

15:32.000 --> 15:36.000
Eviction Lochmaddy, North Uist, in 1891. 

15:37.000 --> 15:41.000
Crofters’ kitchen room in the old style in Lochboisdale. 

15:41.000 --> 15:46.000
Crofters’ living room in the new style, in Callanish in Lewis. 

15:48.000 --> 15:50.000
Isn’t that funny?

15:53.000 --> 15:56.000
Where did you live before you lived here in [Struan/?]?

15:56.000 --> 16:02.000
Oh I lived down in [Milkwater/?], another area just down...

16:02.000 --> 16:06.000
In a wee, in a wee croft? In a cottage?

16:06.000 --> 16:12.000
Oh well it was a… a house just like the, the old-style house from 1930. 

16:12.000 --> 16:15.000
Very like Donald MacRury’s.

16:15.000 --> 16:16.000
Aye, right, aye.

16:17.000 --> 16:20.000
Archie, what was I going to say? No but, do you take milk and sugar?

16:20.000 --> 16:22.000
Aye. Just milk please.

16:22.000 --> 16:23.000
Milk alone?
Aye.

16:26.000 --> 16:31.000
Did you [XX] eat oatcake ever? A bit of oatcake?

16:28.000 --> 16:31.000
Mm, aye. I make really good oatcakes.

16:32.000 --> 16:34.000
You do? You’ve–? Oh well!

16:33.000 --> 16:37.000
Mm, aye. I love oatcakes.

16:39.000 --> 16:40.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

16:40.000 --> 16:45.000
James takes me round Lochskipport. James, Seumas takes me round Lochskipport.

16:45.000 --> 16:47.000
Oh, skipport aye.

16:47.000 --> 16:48.000
His old place.

16:48.000 --> 16:53.000
Oh, I remember James MacLeod, he knew me too.

16:53.000 --> 16:55.000
Aye, so he said. 

16:56.000 --> 17:00.000
