43:30.000 --> 43:34.000
Just a short story about the Titanic. 

43:35.000 --> 43:43.000
And I was in one of the Glasgow boats, the Red Breast was her name, 

43:43.000 --> 43:51.000
and she was one of the boats of the Burn’s Company running between Glasgow and Belfast. 

43:52.000 --> 44:05.000
We were leaving Glasgow in the afternoon, about half past seven at night, 

44:06.000 --> 44:17.000
at Prince’s Pier and we took on board all the what we wanted of fish, boxes of fish. 

44:19.000 --> 44:24.000
And after we left Prince’s Pier in Gourock we were going … 

44:24.000 --> 44:34.000
to the pier in Belfast before the day would break.

44:34.000 --> 44:40.000
And this day there was an awful commotion, 

44:40.000 --> 44:45.000
the Titanic was being launched and we could see her, 

44:45.000 --> 44:51.000
like a big castle on the other side of the river. 

44:51.000 --> 44:57.000
And the way we were working on this boat, 

44:58.000 --> 45:04.000
we had to wash her down in the morning and then go to our breakfast, 

45:04.000 --> 45:14.000
and then we were off for a while. We could go to bed and we could go, go into town. 

45:14.000 --> 45:22.000
And we were putting up some [buoys/?], 

45:23.000 --> 45:28.000
and oh in these days they were taking a lot of cattle over from Ireland to Glasgow. 

45:28.000 --> 45:34.000
And we had to put up these stalls, you know, and put a rope on the stalls so that the cows… 

45:34.000 --> 45:41.000
to keep them separate from the ones who had horns, and the ones who had no horns.

45:42.000 --> 45:50.000
And, but this day, they were launching the Titanic, and nobody went to… 

45:50.000 --> 45:57.000
we all went ashore, nobody went to bed that day, 

45:57.000 --> 46:01.000
and we were down there seeing the launching of the Titanic. 

46:02.000 --> 46:07.000
And, you could get, there was a lot of... 

46:07.000 --> 46:15.000
a lot on the pier where the boat was built and 

46:15.000 --> 46:20.000
we were just waiting so she would go, but… 

46:22.000 --> 46:26.000
a h-uile duine a bh’ air bòrd shìos ann a shiud a’ faighinn, 

46:26.000 --> 46:30.000
what I was saying was every one of them was up there.

46:30.000 --> 46:34.000
And we were waiting in a minute in silence, 

46:35.000 --> 46:40.000
hearing the last stages of the boat being built. 

46:40.000 --> 46:49.000
And then we heard, we heard in English, “there she goes, there she goes”. 

46:49.000 --> 46:51.000
Bha i [dìreadh/?] a’ chiad [gluasad/?] aic’. 

46:51.000 --> 46:54.000
That was her first movement. 

46:54.000 --> 46:58.000
Dol gu seirbheis airson ’s gun [roinneadh/?] i. 

46:58.000 --> 47:00.000
For… for the sea.

47:01.000 --> 47:06.000
Now two old men from Ireland were beside me, 

47:06.000 --> 47:11.000
